      "question": "a) Differentiate programmed I/O and Interrupt briefly.\nb) Describe DMA mode of data transfer in details with suitable diagram.",
      "answer": "**a)** In programmed I/O the CPU repeatedly polls the device status and stays busy during transfer; with interrupts the device signals the CPU when attention is needed, so the CPU can do other work until then.\n\n**b)** The CPU initializes the DMA controller with source, destination, count and direction; the DMAC requests the bus, transfers a block directly between memory and device using address/count registers, raises an interrupt when done, and the CPU regains control. A textual block diagram is: `Device ↔ DMA Controller ↔ System Bus ↔ Memory`, with the CPU connected to the bus and to the DMAC control lines.",
